<%# ONE SAMPLE, IN SEQUENCE: everything that happened between a name and a
    teaser and a room a player could walk into, in the order it happened, each
    step marked with who decided it.

    THE CAPTAIN, 2026-09-07: *"It's still hard for me to hold in my head the full
    sequence of everything that happens each turn or as you move into a new
    room/location."* So this is a COLUMN and not a grid, and the purple/teal marks
    down the left are the README's turn-diagram convention -- purple is a model
    call, teal is the app deciding from records it holds.

    NOTHING HERE IS COMPUTED THAT THE BENCH DOES NOT ALREADY COMPUTE. Every value
    is read off the sample's stored row through
    `Eval::Realization::Scorer::Reading`, which is the one reader of a row in this
    repository -- so this page and `rake eval:realization` cannot come to
    different readings of one call. `Lab::Realization::Sample`'s header says what
    the row holds and why nothing is stored beside it. %>
<% reading = @sample.reading %>
<% facts = reading.facts %>
<%= render "lab/styles" %>
<%# THE MAP PAGE'S OWN DRAWING RULES, rendered here because the floor plan below
    is the map page's own partial. One stylesheet for one renderer. %>
<%= render "map/styles" %>

<h1>a sample &mdash; <%= @kind.name %></h1>
<p class="nav">
  <%= link_to "&larr; the kind".html_safe, lab_kind_path(@kind) %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= link_to "the lab", lab_kinds_path %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @kind.world %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; sample #<%= @sample.id %>,
  drawn <%= @sample.created_at.utc.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M") %> UTC
</p>
<p class="nav">
  <%= pluralize(reading.calls, "call") %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= @sample.row["input_tokens"] %> in / <%= @sample.row["output_tokens"] %> out
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <%= debug_value(@sample.row["seconds"]) %>s
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; answered by <%= debug_value(@sample.row["answered_by"]) %>
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; prompt <%= debug_value(@sample.row["instructions_digest"]) %>
</p>

<% if @sample.failed? %>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3 class="warn">the call failed</h3>
    <pre><%= reading.error %></pre>
    <p class="note">
      A failed call is still a sample and is out of every rate's denominator
      &mdash; <code>Eval::Realization::Scorer#readings</code>' rule, which is what
      stops a run of refusals reading as a run with nothing wrong with it. A
      <code>BaseAgent::RefusalError</code> is the one failure that is about the
      prompt<%= reading.refused? ? ", and this is one" : "" %>.
    </p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<h2>the sequence</h2>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>1 &mdash; the stub, born <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <div class="fields">
    <div class="k">name</div>
    <div class="v"><%= facts["room"] %></div>
    <div class="k">teaser</div>
    <div class="v"><%= facts["teaser"] %></div>
    <div class="k">danger</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= facts["danger"] %>
      <span class="absent">(<%= @kind.danger.present? ? "declared on the kind" : "rolled by Location::Danger.for_a_new_room" %>)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="k">danger share</div>
    <div class="v"><%= debug_value(facts["danger_share"], absent: "none") %></div>
    <div class="k">population word</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= debug_value(@kind.population, absent: "none -- the engine rolls one out of Location::Population::ROLLED when somebody walks in") %>
    </div>
    <div class="k">the way back</div>
    <div class="v"><%= debug_value(facts["reached_from"], absent: "nothing -- an opening room") %></div>
    <div class="k">already reaches</div>
    <div class="v"><%= facts["reachable"].presence&.join(", ") || debug_value(nil, absent: "nothing yet") %></div>
  </div>
  <% if @kind.inside.present? %>
    <p class="note">
      The <code><%= @kind.inside %></code> band on the kind is what a real exits
      call would have picked. The engine rolled the footprint inside it
      (<code>Location::Parameters#footprint</code>, on <code>Roll::FOOTPRINT</code>'s
      own axis), which is what makes <code>Location#place?</code> true and sends
      the detail call <code>Location::PlaceSchema</code> instead.
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3>2 &mdash; what the world held before the call, <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    Four fifths of a realization prompt is the world AROUND the room, and every
    list here was read off the records by the same registries and scopes the
    prompt itself reads &mdash; so nothing below is a second derivation of what
    the model was told.
  </p>
  <div class="fields">
    <div class="k">people allowed</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= reading.people_allowance %>
      <% if reading.parameters_asked? %>
        <span class="absent">(a building is asked for nobody &mdash; nobody stands in a container)</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="k">things allowed</div>
    <div class="v"><%= reading.item_allowance %></div>
    <div class="k">ways out left</div>
    <div class="v"><%= reading.exit_allowance %></div>
    <div class="k">the cast, already rolled</div>
    <div class="v">
      <% if reading.slots.empty? %>
        <%= debug_value(nil, absent: "nobody offered, so nothing was rolled") %>
      <% else %>
        <% reading.slots.each_with_index do |slot, index| %>
          the <%= (index + 1).ordinalize %> is <%= slot["race"] %>, about <%= slot["age"] %>,
          <%= slot["sex"] %><%= slot["monstrous"] ? " (monstrous)" : "" %><br>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="k">already standing here</div>
    <div class="v"><%= reading.already_present.presence&.join(", ") || debug_value(nil, absent: "nobody") %></div>
    <div class="k">asked to name itself</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= reading.asked_for_a_name? ? "yes" : "no" %>
      <% if reading.names_shown.any? %>
        &mdash; shown as taken: <%= reading.names_shown.join(", ") %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="k">monstrous races in this world</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= Array(facts["monstrous_races"]).presence&.join(", ") ||
            debug_value(nil, absent: "none -- a dangerous room here draws an ordinary person") %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <details>
    <summary>every place the exits prompt was offered (<%= reading.places.size %>)</summary>
    <div class="body scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>place</th><th>written</th><th>this room can already reach it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <% reading.places.each do |place| %>
            <tr>
              <td><%= place["name"] %></td>
              <td><span class="tag <%= place["realized"] ? "realized" : "stub" %>"><%= place["realized"] ? "written" : "stub" %></span></td>
              <td><%= place["connected"] ? "yes" : "no" %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>
  <% if reading.planned? %>
    <details>
      <summary>the floor plan this room was handed as fact</summary>
      <div class="body"><pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(reading.plan) %></pre></div>
    </details>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="step model">
  <h3>3 &mdash; the detail call, answered <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    Sent <code><%= reading.parameters_asked? ? "Location::PlaceSchema" : "Location::DetailSchema" %></code>
    &mdash; <code>Location::Generator#detail_schema</code> chooses off
    <code>Location#place?</code>, and a room's schema is built for that room's own
    people count.
  </p>
  <%= render "lab/samples/exchange", call: "detail", sample: @sample %>
</div>

<% if reading.parameters_asked? %>
  <div class="step model">
    <h3>4 &mdash; what kind of building it is, picked <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
    <p class="note">
      Five closed lists, every field optional, and an absent pick falls to the
      quietest option &mdash; which is what the place then became, so the reading
      below is <code>Location::Parameters</code>' own and not a second one.
      Nothing here is a number: the model picks a label and the engine reads the
      table behind it. <span class="pick hit">A hit</span> is a pick inside what
      you said you expect, <span class="pick miss">a miss</span> one outside it,
      and <span class="pick unscored">grey</span> a pick you said nothing about.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>pick</th><th>the model said</th><th>you expect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <% Lab::Realization.picks.reject(&:per_exit?).each do |pick| %>
            <tr>
              <td><%= pick.name.tr("_", " ") %></td>
              <td><%= lab_pick(@sample.picks_for(pick).first, @kind.expects(pick)) %></td>
              <td><%= @kind.expects(pick)&.join(", ") || tag.span("(don't care)", class: "absent") %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <% if reading.parameters.blank? %>
      <p class="warn">
        The block came back empty, so every pick fell to its default &mdash;
        <code>parameters_declined</code>.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="step engine">
    <h3>5 &mdash; the inside, laid out <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
    <p class="note">
      <code>Location::Interior</code> divided the footprint and
      <code>Location::Danger</code> threw for every room as it was born. THE PICK
      MOVES THE ODDS AND THE DIE STILL DECIDES EACH ROOM, which is why a place
      that picked <em>dangerous</em> and rolled quiet rooms is unlucky rather than
      wrong &mdash; and why nothing on this table is scored against your
      expectation.
    </p>
    <% if reading.rooms.empty? %>
      <p class="absent">(no rooms &mdash; the layout wrote none)</p>
    <% else %>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>room</th><th>storey</th><th>at</th><th>paces</th><th>danger</th><th>hazard</th><th>die</th><th>doors</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <% reading.rooms.each do |room| %>
              <tr>
                <td><%= debug_value(room["name"], absent: "unnamed in this set") %></td>
                <td><%= room["storey"] %></td>
                <%# WHERE IT IS, WHICH IS WHAT THE PLAN BELOW IS DRAWN FROM. A set
                    stored before slice 2 has no `x` at all and says so here
                    rather than printing a nil as a nought. %>
                <td>
                  <% if Lab::Realization::Plan.positioned?(room) %>
                    <%= room["x"] %>, <%= room["y"] %>
                  <% else %>
                    <%= debug_value(nil, absent: "not recorded") %>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
                <td><%= room["width"] %> &times; <%= room["depth"] %></td>
                <td><%= room["danger"] %></td>
                <td><%= debug_value(room["hazard"], absent: "none") %></td>
                <td><%= debug_value(room["hazard_die"], absent: "none") %></td>
                <td><%= room["doors"] %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        <%= pluralize(reading.rooms.size, "room") %>,
        <%= reading.storeys_below %> below the ground floor.
      </p>

      <%# THE SHAPE, WHICH IS THE THING THE TABLE ABOVE CANNOT SHOW. The captain,
          2026-09-08, on what slice 2 buys him: he looks at a building's shape
          instead of reading its table. Drawn through `map/_floor_plan` -- the
          map page's own renderer, at the map page's own scale -- off
          `Lab::Realization::Plan`, which reads the stored row and nothing else. %>
      <% if @plan %>
        <%= render "map/floor_plan", interior: @plan %>
        <p class="note">
          Every rectangle above is the <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>,
          <code>width</code> and <code>depth</code> in the row beside it, at
          <code>Story::Map::PACE</code> pixels to the pace &mdash; nothing here is
          re-derived, and a door is drawn only where the layout actually opened
          one. The dashed outline is the rooms' own union rather than a stored
          extent: they tile the footprint exactly
          (<code>Location::Interior</code>), so the union is the plane they were
          divided out of. Every room reads as a stub because that is what a room
          of a building is until somebody walks into it.
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="absent">
          (no floor plan &mdash; this sample was drawn before <code>x</code> and
          <code>y</code> were stored beside the storey and the extent, and a plan
          guessed from an extent with no position would be a picture of a building
          nobody generated. <code>Lab::Realization::Plan</code>'s header has the
          rule and <code>Eval::Realization::Scorer::Reading#records_the_way_back?</code>
          is where it comes from. Draw the kind again and the next one is drawable.)
        </p>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<div class="step model">
  <h3><%= reading.parameters_asked? ? 6 : 4 %> &mdash; the prose, written <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
  <div class="fields">
    <div class="k">the name it proposed</div>
    <div class="v">
      <%= debug_value(reading.proposed_name.presence, absent: "it was not asked to name itself") %>
      <% if reading.asked_for_a_name? %>
        &rarr; the room is called <strong><%= reading.name_after %></strong>
        <% if reading.name_after == facts["room"] %>
          <span class="warn">(the proposal was refused &mdash; it kept its placeholder)</span>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3>description</h3>
  <%= debug_prose(reading.description) %>
  <h3>lore</h3>
  <%= debug_prose(reading.detail["lore"].to_s) %>
</div>

<% unless reading.parameters_asked? %>
  <div class="step model">
    <h3>5 &mdash; the ways out, answered <%= lab_who(:model) %></h3>
    <% if reading.asked_for_exits? %>
      <p class="note">
        Asked in the SAME conversation as the detail call, which is why the
        cheaper call carries the larger input. Two of the fields per exit are
        picks about a place that does not exist yet: whether it has an inside,
        and how populated it is. These two are answered once PER EXIT, so the
        kind's rate counts a sample as a hit only when EVERY exit's pick is in
        the set you allowed.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>exit</th><th>how far</th><th>how</th><th>inside</th><th>population</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <% inside_expected = @kind.expects(Lab::Realization.pick("inside")) %>
            <% population_expected = @kind.expects(Lab::Realization.pick("population")) %>
            <% reading.exits.each do |exit| %>
              <tr>
                <td><%= exit["name"] %></td>
                <td><%= exit["distance"] %></td>
                <td><%= exit["travel_method"] %></td>
                <td><%= lab_pick(Location::Parameters.from("inside" => exit["inside"]).inside, inside_expected) %></td>
                <td><%= lab_pick(exit["population"], population_expected) %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <%= render "lab/samples/exchange", call: "exits", sample: @sample %>
    <% else %>
      <p class="absent">
        (no exits call at all &mdash; this room was already at its cap, so
        <code>Location::Generator#write_exits!</code> asked for nothing. No call,
        no tokens.)
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <div class="step engine">
    <h3>7 &mdash; the ways out, and there was no call, <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
    <p class="absent">
      (a laid-out place's ways out are its ROOMS' ways out &mdash;
      <code>#open_the_way_in!</code> has just moved every doorway it had onto a
      room of it, so <code>#write_exits!</code> returns early and a building costs
      one call rather than two.)
    </p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3><%= reading.parameters_asked? ? 8 : 6 %> &mdash; what the registries made of it, <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    The half no reading of the answer could give. <code>Item::Registry</code> and
    <code>Character::Registry</code> refuse a proposal for reasons the model was
    told about and reasons it was not, and the difference between what was named
    and what was admitted is a defect with a cost.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>the model named</th><th>the records hold</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>people</td>
          <td><%= reading.people.map { |person| person["fullname"] }.compact_blank.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
          <td><%= reading.admitted_people.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>things</td>
          <td><%= reading.items.map { |item| item["name"] }.compact_blank.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
          <td><%= reading.admitted_items.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ways out</td>
          <td><%= reading.exit_names.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
          <td><%= Array(reading.after["exits"]).join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>places that came to exist</td>
          <td><span class="absent">(off the records, not the answer)</span></td>
          <td><%= reading.new_places.join(", ").presence || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <% if Array(@sample.row["missing_fields"]).any? %>
    <p class="warn">a required field never arrived: <%= Array(@sample.row["missing_fields"]).join(", ") %></p>
  <% end %>
  <% if Array(@sample.row["cap_hits"]).any? %>
    <p class="warn">
      a field arrived at its cap, which is the provider cutting the answer off:
      <%= Array(@sample.row["cap_hits"]).join(", ") %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>

<div class="step engine">
  <h3><%= reading.parameters_asked? ? 9 : 7 %> &mdash; what the checks read, <%= lab_who(:engine) %></h3>
  <p class="note">
    <code>Eval::Realization::Scorer</code>, over this one row and nothing else.
    Nearly every check here is a set comparison the app could have made itself; a
    flag is what one of them caught and not a judgement of the room.
  </p>
  <% if @sample.flags.empty? %>
    <p class="absent">(nothing flagged)</p>
  <% else %>
    <ul class="evidence">
      <% @sample.flags.each do |flag| %>
        <li><span class="warn"><%= flag.code %></span> &mdash; <%= flag.evidence %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  <% end %>
</div>

<h2>your verdict</h2>
<p class="note">
  The picks are already scored above, mechanically and for nothing, against what
  you said they should be. This is for what no closed list can state.
  <strong>A <em>good</em> is one click.</strong> The aspects are what let a
  <em>bad</em> grade a particular check later, and zero of them is a complete
  answer.
</p>
<%= render "lab/samples/verdict", sample: @sample %>
